## Stop-the-line decision required

The literal requirement that all 23 constraints have public accept-at-N and
refuse-at-N+1 evidence conflicts with the existing public encoded-object cap for
GitIndex. Clean options:

1. Recommended: accept the honest exception. Keep the 5 MiB public cap, retain the
   direct GitIndex 65,536/65,537 boundary test plus public outer-cap refusal, and
   amend the checklist wording to distinguish validator reachability from public
   representability.
2. Increase the public identity size cap above 12,323,001 bytes. This makes the
   boundary publicly reachable but is a production/spec contract change, violates
   the review's test-only direction, and changes resource-exhaustion behavior.
3. Lower the declared GitIndex entry maximum to a representable number. This is a
   normative specification change outside the task scope.

Exact decision needed: authorize option 1, or explicitly authorize a production and
specification change under option 2 or 3.
# TASK-260830-8x76g1 — logbook (RUN-260901-6ce278)

## 2026-09-01 — recovery source binding

Both supplied archives passed their declared SHA-256 checks and contained only
relative paths. They were unpacked to task-scoped scratch, with accepted-leaves
content overlaid by audit carryforward. The working tree was not overwritten: 56
files already matched, and the remaining nine were later CR revision 10 changes.
A temporary-index tree write proved the checkout exactly matched reviewed candidate
tree `16e1a20bc556f27c581299160e8f4a12ae555bbb` before rework.

## 2026-09-01 — boundary design

The reviewer requested test-only binding for 23 surviving widening mutants. A single
table drives constraints 1–22 through the two public identity entries. Inputs isolate
the intended clause, notably `nodot` for reverse-DNS and `sub\\..\\..\\escape` for
the Windows-separator-only symlink case. Large arrays are valid at the declared cap,
so widening the gate by one admits the over-bound shape and reddens the named test.

## 2026-09-01 — GitIndex representability conflict

The GitIndex closed entry grammar requires eight named members per entry. The valid
65,536-entry parent generated by the boundary test is 12,323,001 encoded bytes. The
public identity path rejects any input above 5,242,880 bytes before closed-shape
validation. Therefore a public accept-at-65,536 test cannot exist without changing
production behavior. The validator itself is pinned at 65,536/65,537 and its widening
mutant is killed; both public entries are additionally proven to refuse the oversized
object. The enumeration was narrowed to this exact evidence.

## 2026-09-01 — validation anomaly and recovery

The first full suite exited 1 because the inventory parser requires the artifact call
site column to equal the `requireExactMembers` owner. Writing “prepareObjectIdentity
then validateGitIndex” caused an intentional drift failure. The call-site column was
restored to `validateGitIndex`, while the outer-gate caveat remained in the constraint
text. The full suite rerun and all subsequent gates exited 0.

## 2026-09-01 — stop-the-line

No validator, limit, or schema was changed to force the impossible public positive.
The recommended resolution is to keep the public cap and accept a checklist exception
that distinguishes direct validator boundary proof from public representability.

